            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="wrenches" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Combination and Ratcheting Wrenches
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Standard Combination Wrenches: Tekton</h3>
        <p>
          This is Tekton's strongest category and the reason the brand has the following it does. The chrome vanadium forgings are clean, the chrome finish is smooth rather than gritty, the wall thickness is consistent, and — the part that actually matters — the jaw and box-end dimensions hold tolerance across a set. Loose-fitting wrenches round fastener flats, and a set where every size fits correctly is worth more in practice than any amount of finish quality.
        </p>
        <p>
          GearWrench's standard combination wrenches are perfectly good, and in a shop that buys from a parts store counter they are the sensible default. But on a like-for-like basis, a full Tekton set typically costs less and gives up very little. Where both brands sit below the premium tier is jaw geometry: neither offers the off-angle designs used by Snap-on and Stahlwille that grip flats rather than corners on damaged hardware, which we cover in our <a href="/tekton-vs-snap-on/">Tekton vs Snap-on comparison</a>.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ratcheting Wrenches: GearWrench, Comfortably</h3>
        <p>
          GearWrench invented the ratcheting combination wrench and it still shows. The ratcheting box ends have a high tooth count and a correspondingly small swing arc, which is exactly the specification that matters in a confined engine bay: with only a few degrees of movement available, a fine mechanism keeps turning where a coarse one simply stalls. The range is also enormous — flex-head, stubby, reversible, offset, metric and SAE — and covers awkward jobs the competition does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tekton does not really compete here. Its ratcheting range is much narrower and it makes no claim to lead the category. If a meaningful part of your work involves fasteners you can barely reach, this single difference outweighs everything else on this page.
        </p>
        <p>
          The caveat worth stating: a ratcheting box end contains a mechanism, and a mechanism is weaker than a solid forged ring. For breaking a seized fastener loose, use a conventional wrench or a breaker bar and save the ratcheting wrench for the fifty turns afterwards. Every ratcheting wrench that dies young was used the other way round.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sockets" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Sockets and Ratchets
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Sockets: Close, With an Edge to Tekton on Price</h3>
        <p>
          Both brands do this properly. Standard sockets are chrome vanadium with a polished chrome finish; impact sockets are chrome molybdenum with a black phosphate coating, which is the correct material for impact duty. Both make the distinction clearly, which matters more than it sounds — using chrome vanadium sockets on an impact gun is a genuine safety issue, and budget brands muddy the line.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tekton's advantage is set pricing and organisation: its socket sets come in well-designed rails and cases, and the cost per socket in a large set is usually lower. GearWrench's advantage is breadth — more specialised sizes, more drive configurations, and the ability to buy the single socket you just lost from a shop five minutes away.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Ratchets: Different Philosophies</h3>
        <p>
          GearWrench ratchets follow the same logic as its wrenches: high tooth counts, small swing arcs, smooth mechanisms. In tight quarters they are excellent, and the feel is closer to premium than the price suggests.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tekton ratchets tend to run coarser and more robust. A lower tooth count means a bigger swing arc and less finesse, but also chunkier pawls that survive abuse. Which you prefer depends genuinely on the work: under a dashboard or in an engine bay, tooth count wins; on a bench or under a mower deck where there is room to swing, durability and price win.
        </p>

        <h2 id="everything-else" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Torque Wrenches, Pliers and the Rest
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Torque Wrenches</h3>
        <p>
          Both brands make click-type torque wrenches that hold industry-standard tolerance through the useful part of their range, and both are far better value than the premium alternatives. Tekton's are the cheaper of the two and have earned a reputation for accuracy that surprises people at the price. GearWrench offers a wider range including electronic and micrometer models, and is easier to source locally when you need one for a specific job tomorrow. Our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench guide</a> covers what accuracy claims actually mean in practice.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Pliers and Screwdrivers</h3>
        <p>
          Honestly, neither brand leads here. Both make competent, serviceable pliers and screwdrivers that are clearly better than big-box house brands and clearly behind the European specialists — Knipex for pliers, Wera and Wiha for drivers. If these are tools you use constantly, buy the specialists for the two or three you reach for most and let either brand cover the rest.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Hex Keys and Specialty Tools</h3>
        <p>
          Tekton's hex and Torx key sets are a quiet strength — well-finished, accurately sized, and sensibly organised in holders that keep them in order. GearWrench's specialty range is broader overall, particularly in automotive-specific tools, which reflects who its customers are.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Breaker Bars and High-Torque Tools</h3>
        <p>
          Tekton breaker bars are one of the quiet bargains in the whole hand tool market: heavy forgings, sensible head geometry, and a price that makes owning both a half-inch and a three-eighths version an easy decision. GearWrench builds equally sound bars and offers more variety in length and flex-head configuration. On this one, buy whichever is in front of you — both will outlast the fastener.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Sets and Storage</h3>
        <p>
          Tekton puts genuine effort into organisers, sockets rails and cases, and it shows in how a set looks after a year of use. GearWrench sets are more often bought as working kits to be tipped into a drawer or a roll, which suits a professional who already has storage sorted.
        </p>

        <h2 id="price-and-buying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Price, Buying and Warranty
        </h2>

        <p>
          On price, Tekton generally wins, and the reason is structural rather than a matter of cheaper steel. Selling direct removes two or three margins from the chain, and that difference shows up most clearly in large sets, where the per-tool cost can be substantially lower than a comparable GearWrench kit.
        </p>

        <p>
          On availability, GearWrench wins just as clearly. Breaking a 15 mm wrench on a Saturday afternoon is a different experience depending on which brand you own: one is a drive to the parts store, the other is an online order and a wait. For anyone whose tools are how they earn a living, that matters more than a percentage on the purchase price.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both offer lifetime warranties on hand tools, but the mechanics differ. Tekton's is claimed directly with the company, needs no receipt, and replacements typically ship within a day or two — it is one of the most consistently praised warranty processes in the category. GearWrench's is honoured through its distribution network, which means the experience depends on where you bought the tool; through a good industrial distributor it is excellent, through a general retailer it can be slower.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Choosing Between Them</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating It as an Either-Or Decision</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These brands are strongest in different places. A box with Tekton combination wrenches, sockets and hex keys alongside a GearWrench ratcheting wrench set is a better outcome than a complete set of either.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Ratcheting Wrenches for Breaking Fasteners Loose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A ratcheting box end is mechanically weaker than a solid one. Crack the fastener with a conventional wrench or breaker bar, then switch to the ratcheting wrench for the turns. Doing it the other way round destroys the mechanism.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dismissing Both Over Country of Manufacture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands manufacture largely in Taiwan and China, as do several premium names. Taiwanese hand tool production has been world-class for decades. The specification and quality control the brand demands is what determines the tool you get.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chasing Tooth Count Everywhere</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fine-tooth ratchet is a real advantage in confined spaces and a slight liability under abuse — more, smaller teeth mean smaller contact area. If your work is on a bench with room to swing, a coarser, tougher mechanism is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring How You Will Replace a Broken Tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both warranties are lifetime, but a warranty is only as useful as its turnaround. If a dead tool stops you earning, local availability is worth paying for; if it is a weekend hobby, direct-to-consumer pricing wins easily.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="who-wins" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Tekton vs GearWrench: Who Wins?</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Choose Tekton if:</strong> you are building or filling out a collection and want the most tool for the money. Its combination wrenches, sockets and hex key sets are excellent for the price, the organisation is genuinely good, and the direct warranty is the least painful in the category. This is the better default for a home shop, a garage, or a first serious set.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Choose GearWrench if:</strong> you need ratcheting wrenches, fine-tooth ratchets, or same-day local availability. In tight automotive work the small swing arc is not a marketing figure, it is the difference between reaching a fastener and not — and being able to replace a broken tool at a parts store counter matters when the tools are how you work.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> The material quality gap between these two is much smaller than the internet suggests, and the specialism gap is much larger. Buy Tekton for the bulk of the box and GearWrench for anything with a ratcheting mechanism in it. That combination costs less than going all-in on either brand and leaves you with better tools in the places it counts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tekton better than GearWrench?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Neither wins across the board. Tekton leads on standard combination wrenches, hex keys and price per tool in sets. GearWrench leads decisively on ratcheting wrenches, which it pioneered, and on fine-tooth ratchets for tight spaces. Most working tool boxes end up holding some of each.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Tekton and GearWrench made in the same place?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both manufacture primarily in Taiwan and China through established contract factories. Taiwan has produced high-grade hand tools for decades. What separates the two brands is the engineering specification, tolerances and quality control each demands — not the address on the box.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which has the better warranty?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both are lifetime on hand tools. Tekton's is faster in practice — claimed directly, no receipt required, replacements usually shipping within a day or two. GearWrench's runs through its distributor and retail network, so the experience varies with where you bought the tool.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is GearWrench worth the extra money over Tekton?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For ratcheting wrenches and fine-tooth ratchets, yes — that mechanism is the brand's core competency and the small swing arc saves real time in confined spaces. For standard wrenches, sockets and hex keys the performance gap is small and Tekton usually costs less.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can you buy each brand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Tekton sells almost exclusively through its own site and Amazon, which is exactly why its prices are lower. GearWrench sells through auto parts stores, industrial distributors and big-box retailers, so replacing a broken tool the same day is realistic.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for a beginner's set?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Tekton, usually. Large sets cost less per tool, the organisers are well thought out, and the warranty is claimed directly with no receipt — which suits someone building a first collection. GearWrench makes more sense once you know which tools you use most and want ratcheting versions of them.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are GearWrench ratcheting wrenches worth it?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In tight spaces, absolutely — a ratcheting box end with a small swing arc turns a barely-reachable fastener into a routine job. Just remember the mechanism is weaker than a solid box end, so break fasteners loose with a conventional wrench first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do professional mechanics use either brand?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Both, usually alongside premium tools. GearWrench has deeper penetration in automotive shops thanks to its ratcheting range and parts-store availability. Tekton is more common with independent technicians and serious home mechanics who buy direct and value the price-to-quality ratio.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

