          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Tekton and GearWrench</strong> are closer in material quality than their positioning suggests and further apart in specialism. Tekton's direct-to-consumer model puts good chrome vanadium in your hand for less money, and its combination wrenches, sockets and hex keys are the best-value part of the comparison. GearWrench owns the ratcheting mechanism and the parts-store shelf.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            So do not pick a side. Build the bulk of the box from Tekton, add GearWrench where a ratcheting head or a fine-tooth mechanism earns its keep, and spend the money you saved on the two or three specialist pliers and drivers that neither brand leads on. That is what most experienced boxes look like once the brand loyalty wears off.
          </p>
